# wireproto

A custom binary message format shared by the server and the client,
with forward/backward interoperability between protocol v1 and v2.

## Frame format

```
+--------+--------+---------+=============+=================+
| magic0 | magic1 | version | body length | fields ...      |
| 0xC5   | 0xA1   | 1 byte  | uvarint     | body length     |
+--------+--------+---------+=============+=================+
```

Each field is a TLV triple:

```
field id  : uvarint, must be > 0
wire type : 1 byte   (0 = varint, 1 = bytes, 2 = string)
value     : uvarint                    (wire type 0)
          | uvarint length + raw bytes (wire types 1 and 2)
```

Field ids are append-only:

| id | field    | type   | since |
|----|----------|--------|-------|
| 1  | ID       | varint | v1    |
| 2  | Name     | string | v1    |
| 3  | Priority | varint | v2 (optional, default 1) |

## Root cause of the v1/v2 interop failure

When the server was upgraded to v2 it started appending the new
optional field `Priority` (id 3) to every frame. Two defects combined
into the observed "field misalignment + occasional panic" on old
clients:

1. **Encoder bug (the planted fault):** the encoding branch for the new
   field wrote a wrong field id / length, so the bytes on the wire did
   not match the documented TLV layout. Any decoder — old or new — that
   trusted those bytes lost track of field boundaries and parsed
   garbage from the middle of the payload.
2. **Decoder fragility:** the v1 decoder assumed it knew every field in
   the frame and indexed into the buffer without bounds checks. An
   unexpected field (or a corrupt length) therefore shifted every
   subsequent field and, for truncated or hostile input, panicked with
   a slice-out-of-range instead of returning an error.

## Compatibility strategy

- **TLV with explicit boundaries.** Every field carries its id, wire
  type, and (for byte payloads) its length, so a decoder can always
  find the next field boundary without understanding the field.
- **Skip-and-preserve unknown fields.** Decoders accept frames from any
  known peer version. Fields with unrecognized ids are skipped via
  their TLV boundary and retained in `Message.Unknown`, so a v1 client
  correctly recovers `ID`/`Name` from a v2 frame and can forward the
  unknown field untouched.
- **Defaults for absent optional fields.** A v2 decoder reading a v1
  frame fills the missing optional field with its documented default
  (`DefaultPriority = 1`).
- **Frozen v1 layout.** Existing field ids, wire types, and byte layout
  never change. Golden sample frames under `testdata/v1/` (constructed
  by hand, independently of the encoder) regress any accidental change
  to the v1 byte format.
- **Frame body length.** The header carries the exact body length, so
  truncation at any byte offset — even exactly at a field boundary —
  is detectable and returns `ErrTruncated`/`ErrTrailing`.
- **Fail, never panic.** All decoding goes through a bounds-checked
  reader. Truncation, length overrun, over-long varints (> 10 bytes),
  field id 0, and unknown wire types all return typed errors.

## Development

```
make test     # unit + interop + malformed + golden tests
make interop  # v1<->v2 matrix and golden-sample regression only
make fuzz     # 100k random inputs (no panic) + 15s go-fuzz session
```
